  "text": "A data analyst interview usually comes in three parts. A SQL screen, live or as a take-home. A few questions on spreadsheets and statistics. And a conversation that starts with: how would you analyze this?\n\nUnder all three, interviewers check four things. SQL fluency. Framing: the right grain, nulls handled, nothing counted twice after a join. Communication: your assumptions, your caveats, and what you'd check next. And tools: a spreadsheet, and a B I tool, short for business intelligence, like Tableau, Power BI or Looker.\n\nA I, artificial intelligence, has changed some of it. You're now as likely to check a query an assistant wrote as to write one. You'll be asked how you use these tools responsibly. B I tools answer questions in plain language, but only on top of clean metrics. And the live SQL round is usually A I-off, so ask the recruiter about each round.\n\nThis series follows the track, one video per topic page, from SQL to A I-assisted analysis: a hundred and fifty-six questions in all. Each video works through the questions that need a figure, then takes the rest quickly. And every page has practice of its own: concept checks, runnable challenges, and a bank of a hundred and ten multiple-choice questions. Almost every interview opens with the same screen. Next: SQL.",
